# Generic IR manipulation

This package implements the third layer independently of the scheduler, semantic contracts and domain implementations. Public exports are in `index.ts`. Domain adapters retain their native representations and expose a finite logical projection for shared algorithms.

## Adapt and open a representation

`defineAdapter(definition)` captures versioned adapter metadata and callbacks from a plain record with own callback properties. Wrap class methods explicitly when adapting an existing class implementation. The mandatory methods are `entities`, `resolve`, `relations` and `describe`; `relationKinds` assigns each named edge kind to containment, dependency, binding, adjacency or provenance. Enumeration and resolution must agree. Duplicate IDs, invalid edge categories, missing endpoints and malformed roots are rejected.

Optional `validate`, `rebuild`, `operation`, `serialize`/`deserialize` and `visualize` functions supply additional capabilities. A rewritable adapter requires a native validator. A serializer and deserializer must be supplied together. Adapter callbacks are synchronous; enumeration may use synchronous iterators. Registration is versioned through `AdapterRegistry`, which rejects changing a registered definition under the same version tuple.

`createIRArtifact(adapter,id,value)` captures finite immutable data and computes its content/revision hashes. `openIR(artifact,adapter,store?)` validates those hashes and binds a view to an exact revision. The optional `IRStore` retains history and owns a compare-and-swap head for each artifact ID. Reopening an existing revision does not advance its head; new revisions are published by edit transactions.

Every reference is `{artifact,revision,entity}`. Methods accept local entity IDs as a convenience, but reject a qualified reference from another artifact or revision. `view.at(committedArtifact)` creates a new view using the same adapter and store. The previous view remains immutable and readable.

## Navigation

`entities`, `resolve`, `describe`, `relations`, `operation`, `find`, `graph`, `serialize` and `visualize` inspect the bound representation. `children`, `parents` and `ancestors` follow containment only. `successors` and `predecessors` follow explicitly named edge kinds. `dependencies` defaults to transitive dependency and binding edges; use `{transitive:false}` for one step.

`walk` supports pre-order, post-order and breadth-first order, selected roots and relation kinds, direction, maximum nodes and maximum depth. It is deterministic and cycle-safe. With no explicit roots it includes disconnected components. Budgets throw `IRBudgetError` instead of returning an incomplete result as success. Default node/depth bounds are 10,000.

`subgraph(selection)` returns the induced logical subgraph. Select `closure:'dependencies'` or `closure:'connected'` for a closure, and optionally restrict its relation kinds and node budget. This is a view projection, not automatically a valid standalone native artifact.

## Transaction lifecycle

`view.beginEdit({label?,verify?})` returns a draft tied to the exact current head and its generation. Available mutations are:

| Method | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `insert(entity)` | Insert a unique stable ID and return a transaction-local handle |
| `replace(target,entity)` | Replace kind/attributes while preserving the entity ID |
| `update(target,attributes)` | Shallow merge attributes |
| `remove(target)` | Remove the entity and its incident relations, without cascading other entities |
| `connect(from,spec,to)` | Add a declared typed relation; supply a stable ID when required by native reconstruction |
| `disconnect(id)` or `disconnect(from,kind,to)` | Remove an exact edge or matching endpoint edges |
| `redirectReferences(from,to,options?)` | Redirect incoming binding/dependency edges by default; other kinds/directions must be explicit |
| `correspond(from,to,relation?)` | Record one-to-one, split, merge, insertion or removal correspondence |

An inserted handle carries the base revision and is valid only inside that draft. It cannot be resolved through the immutable base view. After commit, obtain a reference from the returned revision's view.

`preview()` returns a frozen logical draft. `prepare()` seals mutations, validates the graph, rebuilds native storage, validates its native schema and reprojects the result. It rejects any silent loss or invention of edited logical entities/relations. Native roots are derived by the adapter from the rebuilt value. It then computes actual entity, relation and root changes plus a total correspondence, including implicit retention/addition/removal entries. Preparation does not publish.

`await commit()` optionally verifies the prepared candidate, rechecks the exact head and generation, synchronously accepts semantic state, and publishes the new native revision. The verifier returns `{commit(),rollback()}`; both callbacks must be synchronous. The existing semantic manager's prepared transformation implements that protocol. A rejected candidate never advances the head.

The generation check detects concurrent edits, no-op intervening commits, and an A→B→A content cycle. Native and semantic checks may await before publication, but the final acceptance/publication boundary does not await. Reentrant publication is forbidden. Cancellation through `rollback()` during verification discards subsequent acceptance. A committed draft cannot be reused or rolled back as an undo operation.

Each edit transaction is atomic. A sequence of several committed edits is not automatically one atomic transaction. The rewrite scheduler keeps earlier accepted revisions if a later candidate fails. An enclosing compiler pass has its own publication boundary.

## Differences and correspondence

`diff(beforeView,afterView)` compares logical entities, edge data and roots, including views with different adapters. `affectedEntities(changes)` returns revision-qualified references to changed entities, edge endpoints and changed roots. The edit operation log describes what was requested; the structural difference describes what actually changed.

`makeCorrespondence`, `validateCorrespondence`, `composeCorrespondence`, `invertCorrespondence` and `traceEntity` work independently of a particular native format. A grouped correspondence denotes the Cartesian relation between its source and target IDs. Empty endpoints represent additions or removals. Source and target universes are explicit and fully accounted for.

Composition requires the same intermediate artifact ID, revision and entity universe. Splits and merges compose relationally; creation followed by deletion leaves no surviving endpoint. Inversion reverses historical relations, not the executable edit. Tracing follows an ordered exact-revision chain and retains per-step branching/removal evidence. Default composition and trace bounds are 100,000 pairs/references.

Correspondence records association, not semantic equivalence. A domain checker must justify any preservation or analysis transfer that uses it.

## Patterns and rewriting

See [Patterns and rewrite schedules](PATTERNS-AND-REWRITES.md) for all builders, matching evidence, registries, seven schedules, order selection, budgets, scoped edit authority and failure statuses. Pure pattern/guard callbacks receive a frozen `IRReader`; rewrite callbacks receive a mutation-only `RewriteEdit`. The scheduler retains prepare/commit authority and seals callback access before semantic acceptance.

## Extension and trust boundaries

The package imposes a common logical inspection/edit protocol, not a physical storage model or universal semantic type system. Adapter callbacks, registered predicates, rewrite implementations and semantic verifiers are trusted TypeScript code. Read-only/mutation-only facades constrain the capabilities passed to callbacks; they are not a JavaScript sandbox against code holding unrelated references.

The revision store is in memory. Traces can be exported, but process recovery, distributed transactions, graph indexes, unbounded streaming and persistent cache services are separate concerns. There is no e-graph engine, SSA package, formal prover or automatic derivation of domain invariants.
# Strategy IR v1

This module is the domain-independent orchestration language in workbench v4. It is a complete implementation of the thirteen operators enumerated in the design. The executable plan is immutable, versioned JSON; functions remain in an explicit algorithm registry. The existing compiler remains responsible for task dependencies, output validation, artifact caching and provenance.

## Public API

Import the namespace to avoid confusing `require` or `parallel` with other APIs:

```ts
import * as S from './strategy/index.js';

const registry = new S.AlgorithmRegistry()
  .register({
    id: 'mesh.reduce', version: '1', kind: 'transform',
    description: 'Reduce a mesh with a recorded error bound',
    run: async (ctx, mesh, args) => {
      // An adapter may require an existing compiler task here.
      // ctx.link({taskId, invocationId, cache, artifact}) records that link.
      return reduceMesh(mesh, args);
    }
  });

const plan = S.plan('mesh-plan', S.sequence('main',
  S.apply('reduce', S.algorithm('mesh.reduce', '1'), {
    args: { tolerance: 0.01 }, bind: 'reduced'
  }),
  S.checkpoint('inspect', 'Reduced result', S.binding('reduced'))
));

const execution = await S.execute(plan, registry, mesh, {
  id: 'compilation-1', maxSteps: 10000, maxConcurrency: 4
});
if (execution.status !== 'succeeded') {
  console.error(execution.error, execution.records);
}
```

The example's `reduceMesh` is an application implementation, not an engine builtin. Real domain adapters and plans are in `src/strategy-examples/`.

### Algorithm registry

`new AlgorithmRegistry().register(algorithm)` registers an exact `(id, version)` pair. Registration cannot replace that pair. `get(ref, expectedKind?)`, `has(ref, expectedKind?)`, `list()` and `snapshot()` provide inspection and exact resolution. A plan is checked against all referenced algorithm versions, including unselected branches, before execution. `plan(id, root, description?, requirements?)` may additionally pin transitive host dependencies in its `requirements` manifest. The compiler examples populate this manifest from the complete task graph, so removing a direct `apply` node cannot silently upgrade a pass still required by another task. Intentional algorithm upgrades require updating the explicit version pins.

An algorithm has `id`, `version`, `kind`, `description` and `run(context, input, args)`. Its kind is one of `transform`, `analysis`, `predicate`, `selector`, `invariant` or `metrics`. Inputs, arguments and returned values must be finite, acyclic JSON data. Outputs are copied and frozen. Undefined, functions, nonfinite numbers, classes, accessors and cycles are rejected.

Algorithm context exposes `signal`, `executionId`, `nodeId`, `path`, `link(link)`, `record(label, data)` and `reject(code, message, evidence?)`. It closes when the algorithm returns. A late write throws without mutating the trace. Links identify actual compiler tasks and invocation IDs; cached invocation details can be attached rather than inferred from a plan.

### Values and lexical references

Arguments and explicit inputs are JSON templates. Ordinary objects/arrays are recursively copied. `ref('current', path?)` reads the current flow value; `ref('input', path?)` reads the initial input to the entire execution. `binding(name, path?)` reads a preceding binding. `ref('item')`, `ref('index')` and `ref('iteration')` are available only in their corresponding bodies. Paths use own object keys or array indices. Prototype path segments are forbidden.

`literal(value)` escapes data containing the reserved `$ref` or `$literal` keys. Plans contain no callback expressions. Predicates, selectors, metric computations and custom convergence identities are named versioned algorithms.

Every node may have `bind` and `label`. A binding publishes that node's result in its containing sequence. A sequence's local bindings are available to following steps in that sequence. Child branches, scopes, loop iterations and nested sequences have isolated local frames. To export a nested computation, bind its result on the composite node. `require` additionally creates its named analysis binding while preserving the current flow value.

### Operators

| Builder | Semantics |
| --- | --- |
| `apply(id, algorithm, options?)` | Run a transform on current or explicit `input`, with templated `args`; return its value. |
| `sequence(id, ...steps)` | Feed each result to the next step; execute an empty sequence as identity. |
| `parallel(id, steps, options?)` | Run independent branches from the same immutable input and isolated bindings; return ordered result array. |
| `fork(id, branches, options?)` | Run named alternatives from the same input; retain successful values and explicit rejected candidates in a `strategy.candidates` value. |
| `chooseBest(id, candidates, objective, options?)` | Execute its candidate-producing child; score every successful candidate, filter metric constraints and return the winning value. |
| `conditional(id, predicate, then, otherwise, options?)` | Require a boolean predicate; execute the selected branch and record the other as skipped. |
| `repeatUntilStable(id, body, options)` | Run the body repeatedly, carrying its result forward; stop only on identity equality. Bound is mandatory. Cycles and nonconvergence fail. |
| `forEach(id, body, options)` | Select an ordered item array using `items`, a selector, or current input. Run body on each item with item/index references; return ordered results. `maxItems` is mandatory. |
| `require(id, analysis, as, options?)` | Run an analysis and bind its result under `as`; return current input unchanged. |
| `verify(id, invariant, options?)` | Require boolean or `{ok, reason?, evidence?}`. False creates an explicit recoverable rejection; success preserves current value. |
| `checkpoint(id, name, data?, options?)` | Record data or current value as evidence and preserve current input. |
| `attempt(id, body, fallback, options?)` | Run body; only an explicit rejection matching optional `codes` activates fallback on the original input. |
| `scope(id, name, body, options?)` | Name and isolate a subcomputation; return its value, without leaking its local bindings. |

`chooseBest` options include `metrics`, `args`, `constraints`, `decisionAs`, `bind` and `label`. `decisionAs` publishes the immutable selection evidence in a named lexical binding while the node continues to return the winning value. It must differ from `bind`. Downstream materializers can receive that decision explicitly alongside the selected artifact, keeping attribution correct when several choices inspect the same candidates. Decision data includes the responsible node ID, objective, constraints, winner, alternatives and tie-breaking rule. Execution IDs remain in the trace rather than in compiler cache parameters. The metrics algorithm returns a numeric map. Without it, each candidate value must expose its map as `metrics`. Objective is `{metric, direction:'min'|'max'}`; constraints support `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` and `==` with finite thresholds. Missing/nonfinite metrics fail instead of quietly rejecting a candidate. Equal objectives use lexicographic candidate-name comparison by JavaScript code-unit order, independent of host locale. Rejected and infeasible alternatives, individual violations and winner are all retained.

The candidate child can be any node returning a `CandidateSet`, not just `fork`. This supports an item loop followed by an application algorithm that names its resulting candidates.

`repeatUntilStable` defaults to canonical finite-JSON identity. Artifact-shaped values with `id`, `revision`, `kind`, `schemaVersion` and `value` compare `(kind, schemaVersion, revision)` and ignore logical names. A custom `identity` analysis can project the application's semantic identity. The identity analysis is versioned and traced. Fixpoints are directed bounded iteration, not an e-graph or a theorem prover.

## Failure, concurrency and budgets

`StrategyRejected` is the only recoverable exception channel. Algorithms may use `ctx.reject`; failed invariants and empty feasible sets use it. Ordinary exceptions, invalid algorithm outputs, unavailable references, cycles and exceeded budgets remain fatal. `attempt` does not hide implementation bugs. When parallel siblings fail differently, a fatal failure takes precedence over rejection; explicit cancellation takes precedence over rejection when the external signal is aborted. Within a failure class, source-order index gives deterministic error selection.

`parallel`, `fork` and `forEach` support optional local `concurrency`. `ExecuteOptions.maxConcurrency` limits active algorithm calls globally, including nested groups. Only algorithm calls acquire a permit, so composites never hold a permit while waiting for child work. This is asynchronous scheduling, not CPU worker parallelism.

All started operations are awaited after failure. Unscheduled siblings are marked skipped while the trace budget permits. There is no rollback: successful compiler tasks can remain cached even if a later alternative rejects or execution fails. That retained work remains observable.

`maxSteps` counts every allocated node visit, including skipped subtree records, and therefore bounds trace-record allocation. A limit failure does not allocate records for remaining nodes; the nearest recorded ancestor explains the budget failure. `maxDepth` bounds execution nesting; plans are also limited to 10,000 static nodes and static depth 128. `maxItems` and `maxIterations` are required at the corresponding nodes. These are structural budgets, not memory/process isolation or wall-clock preemption. Algorithm evidence payload sizes remain the host's responsibility.

Cancellation is cooperative. The interpreter checks `AbortSignal` between nodes and before/after algorithms and cancels queued semaphore requests. Long asynchronous algorithms must honor `ctx.signal`; synchronous JavaScript cannot be preempted by this interpreter. No fallback catches cancellation. Already-started host work is awaited, not abandoned with an unhandled rejection.

## Validation, editing and interchange

- `validate(value, registry?)` returns `{valid, issues}` with paths and codes. It checks JSON shape, format version, exact fields, IDs, references, lexical binding availability, algorithm categories/versions and numeric bounds.
- `assertValid(value, registry?)` throws `PlanValidationError` with the same issues.
- `walk(plan, visitor?)` returns ordered `{node,parent?,path,depth,slot?}` visits and optionally calls `visitor(node, visit)` in preorder; `find(plan,id)` or `find(plan,predicate)` returns the first matching node or undefined (`predicate(node, visit)`); `childNodes(node)` exposes direct structural children.
- `replace(plan,id,node)`, `insertBefore(plan,id,node)`, `insertAfter(plan,id,node)` and `remove(plan,id)` return new immutable validated plans. Invalid operations leave the old plan untouched. Insertions create a fresh sequence when the target has no sequence parent. Removal supports list entries; a required child or root must be replaced explicitly (an empty sequence is identity).
- `diff(before,after)` reports added, removed, changed and moved nodes by stable node ID. Composite changes refer to child IDs rather than duplicating entire descendant trees. Plan metadata changes use the `$plan` pseudo-ID.
- `serialize(plan, pretty=true)` uses deterministic key ordering; `deserialize(text, registry?)` validates and freezes the JSON interchange value. The workbench has its own lossless YAML view/editor; persisted plan bytes remain normal data.

## Execution evidence

`execute(plan, registry, input, options?)` preflights the plan, snapshots it and the registry, then returns a `StrategyExecution` for succeeded, rejected, failed or cancelled execution. Invalid plans fail preflight with `PlanValidationError` and do not run any algorithm. `executeOrThrow` wraps a nonsuccessful execution in `StrategyExecutionError`, retaining the complete `execution` property.

The execution contains the exact immutable plan, SHA-256 plan revision, algorithm manifest, input, terminal result/error, node records and an event stream. Each dynamic record has its own execution ID, stable plan node ID, parent execution ID, deterministic structural path, iteration/item/branch location, input/output, status, child IDs, evidence, timestamps and compiler invocation/cache links. A repeated or mapped static node has multiple dynamic records; inactive paths are explicitly skipped. Selection, predicates, fixpoint comparisons, checks and recovery decisions are recorded at the responsible nodes.

Pass an explicit execution ID to correlate compiler runs. Otherwise, IDs combine the plan revision and a process-local run counter. Timings describe elapsed inclusive wall time, not exclusive CPU profiling.

`onEvent` receives frozen events. If an observer throws, its error is recorded as `observer-error` evidence and the observer is disabled; compiler semantics remain unchanged. Returned traces are immutable and retain this diagnostic.

## Scope boundary

This module does not implement the separately proposed semantic property/contract system or generic IR rewriting framework. It invokes existing transformations through a clean registry, and composes, edits, validates and explains their strategies. Domain algorithm correctness and semantic equivalence remain the domain compiler's responsibility.

 succeeded in 362ms:
/**
 * IRWB-001 E3: passes with declared needs, writes and preservation, applied through v6's machinery.
 *
 * A pass is versioned code that turns one artifact revision into the next. It declares:
 *   - `needs`: analyses whose results it reads (v6 analyses, e.g. dataflow facts), computed for the exact input;
 *   - `writes`: the entity and relation kinds it may change; any other change is `undeclaredWrite`;
 *   - `preserves`: analyses whose results it claims survive. Each claim is checked: first by the analysis's own
 *     transfer verifier (E4: unchanged reads), otherwise by recomputing on the output and comparing. A claim that
 *     neither confirms is `preserveViolated`, and the pass is not published;
 *   - `contract`: an optional v6 transformation contract the output must satisfy before it is accepted.
 *
 * A pass edits either through a v6 `IREdit` on the logical projection (generic, pattern-friendly), or by returning
 * a new native value with explicit correspondence entries (efficient for domain code). Either way the framework
 * derives the v6 change set by diffing the two projections, validates the native value through the adapter, builds
 * the correspondence, enforces `writes`, runs the contract, and checks the preservation claims. Nothing is
 * published unless all of that succeeds.
 */
import type { Artifact } from '../core/types.js';
import { canonical } from '../core/data.js';
import { createIRArtifact, openIR } from '../ir/index.js';
import { diff } from '../ir/diff.js';
import { makeCorrespondence } from '../ir/correspondence.js';
import type {
  ChangeSet,
  CorrespondenceEntry,
  EntityCorrespondence,
  IRAdapter,
  IREdit,
  IRStore,
  IRView,
} from '../ir/index.js';
import type {
  AnalysisDefinition,
  AnalysisRecord,
  Correspondence,
  IRType,
  Ports,
  TransformationDefinition,
} from '../contracts/types.js';
import type { AnalysisManager } from '../contracts/analysis-manager.js';

export class PassError extends Error {
  constructor(
    readonly code: 'undeclaredWrite' | 'preserveViolated' | 'contractRejected' | 'badResult' | 'passFailed',
    message: string,
    readonly evidence?: unknown,
  ) {
    super(`[${code}] ${message}`);
    this.name = 'PassError';
  }
}

export type Needs = Readonly<Record<string, AnalysisDefinition<any, any>>>;
export type FactValues<N extends Needs> = {
  readonly [K in keyof N]: N[K] extends AnalysisDefinition<any, infer R> ? R : never;
};

export interface PassRunContext<S, N extends Needs, P> {
  readonly before: Artifact<S>;
  readonly view: IRView<S>;
  readonly facts: FactValues<N>;
  readonly records: { readonly [K in keyof N]: AnalysisRecord<unknown> };
  readonly params: P;
  /** A v6 edit on the logical projection; used when `run` returns nothing. */
  readonly edit: IREdit<S>;
}

/** A native result: the new value, and how its entities relate to the input's (merge, split, replace…). */
export interface NativeResult<S> {
  readonly native: S;
  readonly correspondence?: readonly CorrespondenceEntry[];
  /** How many edits the pass made, for reporting (Compiler Workbench shows edits per pass). */
  readonly edits?: number;
  /** Domain report (e.g. the edit records of a Compiler Workbench revision), passed through to the result. */
  readonly report?: unknown;
}

export interface PassSpec<S, N extends Needs, P = unknown> {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly version: string;
  readonly description: string;
  readonly ir: IRType<S>;
  readonly adapter: IRAdapter<S>;
  readonly needs?: N;
  /** Entity kinds (glob `prefix*`) and relation kinds the pass may change. */
  readonly writes: { readonly entities: readonly string[]; readonly relations: readonly string[] };
  readonly preserves?: readonly AnalysisDefinition<any, any>[];
  readonly contract?: TransformationDefinition<Ports, Ports>;
  /** Extra contract inputs beyond `input` (e.g. `origin`: r0), supplied by the caller per application. */
  run(ctx: PassRunContext<S, N, P>): void | NativeResult<S> | Promise<void | NativeResult<S>>;
}

export type Pass<S, N extends Needs = Needs, P = unknown> = Readonly<PassSpec<S, N, P>> & {
  readonly category: 'pass';
};

export function definePass<S, N extends Needs = {}, P = unknown>(spec: PassSpec<S, N, P>): Pass<S, N, P> {
  if (!spec.id || !spec.version) throw new Error('A pass needs an id and a version');
  if (typeof spec.run !== 'function') throw new Error(`Pass ${spec.id} needs run`);
  if (!spec.writes || !Array.isArray(spec.writes.entities) || !Array.isArray(spec.writes.relations))
    throw new Error(`Pass ${spec.id} must declare writes { entities, relations }`);
  return Object.freeze({
    ...spec,
    needs: spec.needs ?? ({} as N),
    preserves: spec.preserves ?? [],
    category: 'pass' as const,
  });
}

export interface ApplyOptions<P> {
  readonly manager: AnalysisManager;
  readonly store?: IRStore;
  readonly params?: P;
  /** Additional contract inputs, e.g. `{ origin: r0 }` for a check against the original program. */
  readonly contractInputs?: Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;
  /** Artifact id of the output (default: the input's). */
  readonly outputId?: string;
  /** A contract for this application, overriding the pass's own (e.g. one carrying a project's case inputs). */
  readonly contract?: TransformationDefinition<Ports, Ports>;
  /**
   * Check every preservation claim, computing the fact on the input first when the manager does not hold it.
   * By default a claim is checked only when the fact exists (there is otherwise nothing to keep); auditing
   * makes every application a test of the pass's declarations.
   */
  readonly auditPreservation?: boolean;
}

export interface PreservationOutcome {
  readonly analysis: string;
  readonly how: 'transferred' | 'confirmed-by-recompute';
  readonly evidence: unknown;
}

export interface PassResult<S> {
  readonly pass: { readonly id: string; readonly version: string };
  readonly changed: boolean;
  readonly before: Artifact<S>;
  readonly artifact: Artifact<S>;
  readonly changes?: ChangeSet;
  readonly correspondence?: EntityCorrespondence;
  readonly edits: number;
  readonly preserved: readonly PreservationOutcome[];
  readonly contract?: unknown;
  readonly report?: unknown;
}

const glob = (patterns: readonly string[], kind: string) =>
  patterns.some(p => (p.endsWith('*') ? kind.startsWith(p.slice(0, -1)) : p === kind));

/** The changes a pass made outside what it declared it writes. */
export function undeclaredWrites(
  writes: PassSpec<any, any>['writes'],
  changes: ChangeSet,
): readonly string[] {
  const out = new Set<string>();
  for (const c of changes.entities)
    for (const e of [c.before, c.after])
      if (e && !glob(writes.entities, e.kind)) out.add(`${c.kind} entity ${c.entity} (${e.kind})`);
  for (const c of changes.relations)
    for (const r of [c.before, c.after])
      if (r && !glob(writes.relations, r.kind)) out.add(`${c.kind} relation ${c.relation} (${r.kind})`);
  return [...out].sort();
}

/**
 * v6's semantic correspondence record for an entity correspondence between two revisions. The analysis manager
 * matches records to a contract's `correspondence` requirements by `id` and `relation`, so applyPass builds one
 * record per requirement (default: `entities` / `generic-ir-edit`, as v6's own labs name it).
 */
export function semanticCorrespondence(
  c: EntityCorrespondence,
  id = 'entities',
  relation = 'generic-ir-edit',
): Correspondence {
  return {
    id,
    from: { artifact: c.from.artifact, revision: c.from.revision },
    to: { artifact: c.to.artifact, revision: c.to.revision },
    relation,
    entities: c.entries.map(e => ({ from: e.from, to: e.to })),
    evidence: {
      formatVersion: c.formatVersion,
      relations: c.entries.map(e => e.relation),
      sourceEntities: c.sourceEntities,
      targetEntities: c.targetEntities,
    },
  };
}

export async function applyPass<S, N extends Needs, P>(
  pass: Pass<S, N, P>,
  before: Artifact<S>,
  options: ApplyOptions<P>,
): Promise<PassResult<S>> {
  const { manager } = options;
  const view = openIR(before, pass.adapter, options.store);
  const facts: Record<string, unknown> = {},
    records: Record<string, AnalysisRecord<unknown>> = {};
  for (const [name, def] of Object.entries(pass.needs ?? {})) {
    const record = await manager.analyse(def, before);
    records[name] = record;
    facts[name] = record.value;
  }
  const edit = view.beginEdit();
  let produced: void | NativeResult<S>;
  try {
    produced = await pass.run({
      before,
      view,
      facts: facts as FactValues<N>,
      records: records as PassRunContext<S, N, P>['records'],
      params: options.params as P,
      edit,
    });
  } catch (e) {
    if (edit.state === 'open') edit.rollback('pass threw');
    throw e;
  }
  let after: Artifact<S>, changes: ChangeSet, correspondence: EntityCorrespondence, edits: number;
  let report: unknown;
  if (produced && typeof produced === 'object' && 'native' in produced) {
    if (edit.state === 'open') edit.rollback('native result');
    const id = options.outputId ?? before.id;
    after = createIRArtifact(pass.adapter, id, produced.native);
    const afterView = openIR(after, pass.adapter, options.store);
    changes = diff(view, afterView);
    correspondence = makeCorrespondence(
      { artifact: before.id, revision: before.revision },
      { artifact: after.id, revision: after.revision },
      // the projections' entity ids, in order (what `view.entities()` names, without a reference per entity)
      view.graph().entities.map(e => e.id),
      afterView.graph().entities.map(e => e.id),
      produced.correspondence ?? [],
    );
    edits = produced.edits ?? changes.entities.length;
    report = produced.report;
  } else {
    if (edit.state !== 'open') throw new PassError('badResult', `Pass ${pass.id} closed its edit itself`);
    if (edit.operationCount === 0) {
      // IRWB-004: an edit with no operations is the identity (the projection and rebuild are inverses), so preparing
      // it would rebuild, re-create and re-diff the unchanged program only to find nothing changed
      edit.rollback('no operations');
      return {
        pass: { id: pass.id, version: pass.version },
        changed: false,
        before,
        artifact: before,
        edits: 0,
        preserved: [],
      };
    }
    const prepared = edit.prepare();
    after = prepared.artifact;
    changes = prepared.changes;
    correspondence = prepared.correspondence;
    edits = changes.operations.filter(o => o.kind !== 'correspond').length;
    edit.rollback('applyPass publishes revisions itself');
  }
  if (after.revision === before.revision && !changes.entities.length && !changes.relations.length)
    return {
      pass: { id: pass.id, version: pass.version },
      changed: false,
      before,
      artifact: before,
      edits: 0,
      preserved: [],
    };

  const undeclared = undeclaredWrites(pass.writes, changes);
  if (undeclared.length)
    throw new PassError('undeclaredWrite', `Pass ${pass.id} changed kinds it did not declare in writes`, {
      undeclared: undeclared.slice(0, 20),
    });

  let contract: unknown;
  const gate = options.contract ?? pass.contract;
  if (gate) {
    try {
      const prepared = await manager.verifyTransformation(
        { input: before, ...(options.contractInputs ?? {}) } as never,
        { output: after } as never,
        gate,
        gate.correspondence.length
          ? gate.correspondence.map(req =>
              semanticCorrespondence(correspondence, req.id, req.relation),
            )
          : [semanticCorrespondence(correspondence)],
      );
      contract = prepared;
    } catch (e) {
      throw new PassError('contractRejected', `Pass ${pass.id} output rejected: ${(e as Error).message}`, {
        cause: String(e),
      });
    }
  }

  const preserved: PreservationOutcome[] = [];
  for (const def of pass.preserves ?? []) {
    if (!manager.hasAnalysis(def, before)) {
      if (!options.auditPreservation) continue; // nothing to keep
      await manager.analyse(def, before);
    }
    try {
      const record = await manager.preserve(
        def,
        before,
        after,
        semanticCorrespondence(correspondence),
      );
      preserved.push({ analysis: def.id, how: 'transferred', evidence: record.transfer?.evidence });
    } catch {
      const old = await manager.analyse(def, before),
        fresh = await manager.analyse(def, after);
      if (canonical(old.value) !== canonical(fresh.value))
        throw new PassError(
          'preserveViolated',
          `Pass ${pass.id} claims to preserve ${def.id}, but it changed`,
          {
            analysis: def.id,
          },
        );
      preserved.push({ analysis: def.id, how: 'confirmed-by-recompute', evidence: { equalValue: true } });
    }
  }
  return {
    pass: { id: pass.id, version: pass.version },
    changed: true,
    before,
    artifact: after,
    changes,
    correspondence,
    edits,
    preserved,
    contract,
    report,
  };
}
/**
 * IRWB-001 E6: passes and analyses as Strategy IR algorithms.
 *
 * A strategy plan refers to algorithms by id and exact version. `passAlgorithm` registers a pass as a `transform`
 * whose current value is an artifact: applying it returns the next revision (or the same artifact when nothing
 * changed). `analysisAlgorithm` registers an analysis as an `analysis` algorithm, and `metricsAlgorithm` exposes a
 * numeric summary for `chooseBest`. The strategy interpreter records the plan, branches and selections; the pass
 * results themselves are reported through `onResult` so a host (the workbench's revision timeline) can keep them.
 */
import type { Artifact } from '../core/types.js';
import type { AnalysisManager } from '../contracts/analysis-manager.js';
import type { AnalysisDefinition } from '../contracts/types.js';
import type { IRStore } from '../ir/index.js';
import type { StrategyAlgorithm } from '../strategy/ir.js';
import { applyPass, PassError, type Needs, type Pass, type PassResult } from './define-pass.js';

export interface BridgeOptions {
  readonly manager: AnalysisManager;
  readonly store?: IRStore;
  readonly contractInputs?: (input: Artifact<any>) => Readonly<Record<string, unknown>>;
  /** A contract for every application, overriding the passes' own (see ApplyOptions.contract). */
  readonly contract?: import('../contracts/types.js').TransformationDefinition<any, any>;
  readonly onResult?: (result: PassResult<any>) => void;
}

const isArtifact = (x: unknown): x is Artifact<any> =>
  !!x && typeof x === 'object' && typeof (x as Artifact).revision === 'string' && 'value' in (x as object);

export function passAlgorithm<S>(pass: Pass<S, Needs, any>, options: BridgeOptions): StrategyAlgorithm {
  return {
    id: pass.id,
    version: pass.version,
    kind: 'transform',
    description: pass.description,
    async run(ctx, input, args) {
      if (!isArtifact(input))
        ctx.reject('not-an-artifact', `${pass.id} needs an artifact as its current value`);
      try {
        const result = await applyPass(pass, input as Artifact<S>, {
          manager: options.manager,
          store: options.store,
          params: args,
          contractInputs: options.contractInputs?.(input as Artifact<S>),
          contract: options.contract,
        });
        options.onResult?.(result);
        ctx.record('pass', {
          pass: pass.id,
          changed: result.changed,
          edits: result.edits,
          revision: result.artifact.revision,
        });
        return result.artifact;
      } catch (e) {
        // a rejected contract is an expected, recoverable outcome a strategy can handle (attempt, fork)
        if (e instanceof PassError && e.code === 'contractRejected')
          ctx.reject('contract-rejected', e.message, e.evidence);
        throw e;
      }
    },
  };
}

export function analysisAlgorithm<S, R>(
  def: AnalysisDefinition<S, R>,
  options: { manager: AnalysisManager },
): StrategyAlgorithm {
  return {
    id: def.id,
    version: def.version,
    kind: 'analysis',
    description: def.description,
    async run(ctx, input) {
      if (!isArtifact(input)) ctx.reject('not-an-artifact', `${def.id} needs an artifact`);
      return (await options.manager.analyse(def, input as Artifact<S>)).value;
    },
  };
}

export function metricsAlgorithm(
  id: string,
  version: string,
  description: string,
  measure: (value: unknown) => Readonly<Record<string, number>> | Promise<Readonly<Record<string, number>>>,
): StrategyAlgorithm {
  return { id, version, kind: 'metrics', description, run: (_ctx, input) => measure(input) };
}
/**
 * IRWB-001 E5: differential checkers as v6 invariants.
 *
 * Compiler Workbench checks meaning by running two artifacts on the same generated inputs and comparing results,
 * traps and memory. v6 knows only exact checkers. `differential()` packages the sampled method as an ordinary
 * `InvariantDefinition`, so a transformation contract can `ensure` it like any other invariant. Its evidence records
 * the method (`sampled`), the case count and seed, how many cases agreed, disagreed or were inconclusive, and the
 * first counterexamples. Inconclusive cases (for example a step limit hit by either side) are counted and never
 * treated as a verdict; a check with no decided case fails, because it has established nothing.
 */
import { defineInvariant } from './definitions.js';
import type { CheckResult, InvariantDefinition, PortValue, PortValues, Ports } from './types.js';

/** A small deterministic generator (mulberry32), so a reported counterexample can be run again from its seed. */
export function seededRandom(seed: number): () => number {
  let a = seed >>> 0;
  return () => {
    a = (a + 0x6d2b79f5) >>> 0;
    let t = a;
    t = Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 15), t | 1);
    t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61);
    return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;
  };
}

export interface CaseComparison {
  readonly ok: boolean;
  readonly reason?: string;
}

export interface DifferentialSpec<P extends Ports, Case, Outcome> {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly version: string;
  readonly description: string;
  readonly inputs: P;
  /** The port whose behaviour defines the expected meaning (e.g. `origin`, r0). */
  readonly baseline: keyof P & string;
  /** The port being checked against it (e.g. `candidate`). */
  readonly subject: keyof P & string;
  readonly count: number;
  readonly seed?: number;
  /** The generator to seed (default `seededRandom`, mulberry32); a port may supply its tool's own generator. */
  readonly random?: (seed: number) => () => number;
  /** Generate the cases, deterministically from `random` and the inputs. */
  cases(inputs: PortValues<P>, random: () => number, count: number): readonly Case[];
  /** Run one side on one case. `side` names which port `value` came from. */
  run(side: 'baseline' | 'subject', value: PortValue<P[keyof P]>, kase: Case, inputs: PortValues<P>): Outcome;
  compare(baseline: Outcome, subject: Outcome, kase: Case): CaseComparison | boolean;
  inconclusive?(outcome: Outcome): boolean;
  describeCase?(kase: Case): unknown;
  readonly maxCounterexamples?: number;
  /**
   * IRWB-004 D1: a key such that the cases, and the baseline's outcome on each, are a function of this spec (its id,
   * version, seed and count) and the key alone; for a check against a fixed origin, the origin's revision. With a
   * key, both are computed once per key and reused by every later check.
   */
  readonly baselineKey?: (inputs: PortValues<P>) => string | undefined;
  /**
   * IRWB-004 D1: a key such that the subject's outcome on a case is a function of the case and the key alone (for a
   * program, a hash of the code the case can reach). Used only together with `baselineKey`: a decided verdict is
   * kept under (baseline key, case index, subject key) and replayed instead of evaluating the case again.
   */
  readonly subjectKey?: (subject: PortValue<P[keyof P]>, kase: Case, inputs: PortValues<P>) => string | undefined;
  /**
   * IRWB-004 F4: decide the cases at the indices `todo` elsewhere (e.g. on worker threads), returning one verdict
   * per index, in the order of `todo`, exactly as `run`, `inconclusive` and `compare` would decide them; or
   * `undefined` to decide them here. The check's evidence is assembled in case order, whoever decided a case.
   */
  readonly decide?: (
    inputs: PortValues<P>,
    cases: readonly Case[],
    todo: readonly number[],
  ) => Promise<readonly Decided[]> | undefined;
}

/** One decided case: what `compare` (or `inconclusive`) concluded. */
export type Decided =
  | { readonly v: 'agree' }
  | { readonly v: 'inconclusive' }
  | { readonly v: 'disagree'; readonly reason: string };

export interface DifferentialEvidence {
  readonly method: 'sampled';
  readonly invariant: string;
  readonly seed: number;
  readonly total: number;
  readonly agree: number;
  readonly disagree: number;
  readonly inconclusive: number;
  readonly status: 'agree' | 'disagree' | 'inconclusive';
  readonly counterexamples: readonly { readonly case: unknown; readonly reason: string }[];
}

export type DifferentialInvariant<P extends Ports> = InvariantDefinition<P> & {
  readonly method: 'sampled';
};

export function differential<P extends Ports, Case, Outcome>(
  spec: DifferentialSpec<P, Case, Outcome>,
): DifferentialInvariant<P> {
  if (!(spec.count > 0 && Number.isInteger(spec.count)))
    throw new Error('Differential check needs a positive case count');
  if (!(spec.baseline in spec.inputs) || !(spec.subject in spec.inputs) || spec.baseline === spec.subject)
    throw new Error('Differential check needs distinct baseline and subject ports');
  const seed = spec.seed ?? 1,
    maxCx = spec.maxCounterexamples ?? 5;
  const result = (evidence: DifferentialEvidence): CheckResult => ({
    ok: evidence.status === 'agree',
    reason:
      evidence.status === 'agree'
        ? `${evidence.agree}/${evidence.total} sampled cases agree (${evidence.inconclusive} inconclusive)`
        : evidence.status === 'disagree'
          ? `${evidence.disagree}/${evidence.total} sampled cases disagree`
          : `no decided case among ${evidence.total} (all inconclusive)`,
    evidence,
  });
  const inv = defineInvariant<P>({
    id: spec.id,
    version: spec.version,
    description: spec.description,
    inputs: spec.inputs,
    check(_ctx, inputs): CheckResult | Promise<CheckResult> {
      if (spec.decide) return runDifferentialAsync(spec, inputs, seed, maxCx).then(result);
      return result(runDifferential(spec, inputs, seed, maxCx));
    },
  });
  const out = Object.assign(Object.create(null), inv, { method: 'sampled' as const }) as DifferentialInvariant<P>;
  SPECS.set(out, spec as DifferentialSpec<any, any, any>);
  return out;
}

const SPECS = new WeakMap<object, DifferentialSpec<any, any, any>>();
/** IRWB-004 F4: the specification a differential invariant was made from (a worker decides cases with it). */
export function differentialSpec<P extends Ports>(inv: DifferentialInvariant<P>): DifferentialSpec<P, any, any> {
  const spec = SPECS.get(inv);
  if (!spec) throw new Error(`${inv.id} is not a differential invariant of this realm`);
  return spec;
}

/**
 * IRWB-004 D1: what a check against a baseline key keeps. The cases and the baseline's outcomes (computed as cases
 * are first evaluated) are fixed by the key; `verdicts` maps `index \0 subject key` to the decided verdict. A few
 * keys are kept, least recently used first out.
 */
interface Reuse {
  readonly cases: readonly unknown[];
  readonly outcomes: unknown[];
  readonly verdicts: Map<string, Decided>;
}
const REUSE = new Map<string, Reuse>();
const REUSE_MAX = 4;
let reuseEnabled = true;
/** Turn the reuse of cases, baseline outcomes and verdicts on or off (tests and measurements compare both). */
export function setDifferentialReuse(enabled: boolean): void {
  reuseEnabled = enabled;
  if (!enabled) REUSE.clear();
}
/** Counts since the last reset: sides run here, verdicts replayed from the memo, cases decided by `decide`. */
const STATS = { baselineRuns: 0, subjectRuns: 0, replayed: 0, delegated: 0 };
export function differentialStats(reset = false): Readonly<typeof STATS> {
  const out = { ...STATS };
  if (reset) for (const k of Object.keys(STATS) as (keyof typeof STATS)[]) STATS[k] = 0;
  return out;
}

function prepare<P extends Ports, Case, Outcome>(
  spec: DifferentialSpec<P, Case, Outcome>,
  inputs: PortValues<P>,
  seed: number,
): { cases: readonly Case[]; reuse?: Reuse } {
  const bk = reuseEnabled ? spec.baselineKey?.(inputs) : undefined;
  if (bk === undefined) return { cases: spec.cases(inputs, (spec.random ?? seededRandom)(seed), spec.count) };
  const key = `${spec.id}@${spec.version}#${seed}#${spec.count}#${bk}`;
  let reuse = REUSE.get(key);
  if (reuse) REUSE.delete(key);
  else
    reuse = {
      cases: spec.cases(inputs, (spec.random ?? seededRandom)(seed), spec.count),
      outcomes: [],
      verdicts: new Map(),
    };
  REUSE.set(key, reuse);
  while (REUSE.size > REUSE_MAX) REUSE.delete(REUSE.keys().next().value!);
  return { cases: reuse.cases as readonly Case[], reuse };
}

const AGREE: Decided = Object.freeze({ v: 'agree' }),
  INCONCLUSIVE: Decided = Object.freeze({ v: 'inconclusive' });
/**
 * Decide one case: run both sides, then `inconclusive`, then `compare`, as a check always has. `outcomes`, when
 * given, keeps the baseline's outcome by case index (it is fixed by the baseline key); a worker deciding cases for a
 * check (IRWB-004 F4) calls this too, with its own `outcomes`.
 */
export function decideCase<P extends Ports, Case, Outcome>(
  spec: DifferentialSpec<P, Case, Outcome>,
  inputs: PortValues<P>,
  kase: Case,
  index: number,
  outcomes?: unknown[],
): Decided {
  let a = outcomes?.[index] as Outcome | undefined;
  if (a === undefined) {
    a = spec.run('baseline', inputs[spec.baseline] as PortValue<P[keyof P]>, kase, inputs);
    STATS.baselineRuns++;
    if (outcomes) outcomes[index] = a;
  }
  STATS.subjectRuns++;
  const b = spec.run('subject', inputs[spec.subject] as PortValue<P[keyof P]>, kase, inputs);
  if (spec.inconclusive?.(a) || spec.inconclusive?.(b)) return INCONCLUSIVE;
  const c = spec.compare(a, b, kase);
  const verdict = typeof c === 'boolean' ? { ok: c } : c;
  return verdict.ok ? AGREE : Object.freeze({ v: 'disagree', reason: verdict.reason ?? 'outcomes differ' });
}

/** The evidence of decided cases, counted in case order (so counterexamples come in case order). */
function tally<P extends Ports, Case, Outcome>(
  spec: DifferentialSpec<P, Case, Outcome>,
  seed: number,
  cases: readonly Case[],
  verdicts: readonly Decided[],
  maxCounterexamples: number,
): DifferentialEvidence {
  let agree = 0,
    disagree = 0,
    inconclusive = 0;
  const counterexamples: { case: unknown; reason: string }[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < cases.length; i++) {
    const d = verdicts[i];
    if (d.v === 'inconclusive') inconclusive++;
    else if (d.v === 'agree') agree++;
    else {
      disagree++;
      if (counterexamples.length < maxCounterexamples)
        counterexamples.push({
          case: spec.describeCase ? spec.describeCase(cases[i]) : cases[i],
          reason: d.reason,
        });
    }
  }
  return {
    method: 'sampled',
    invariant: spec.id,
    seed,
    total: cases.length,
    agree,
    disagree,
    inconclusive,
    status: disagree ? 'disagree' : agree ? 'agree' : 'inconclusive',
    counterexamples,
  };
}

/** The memo keys of the cases (undefined where a case has no subject key or nothing is reused). */
function memoKeys<P extends Ports, Case, Outcome>(
  spec: DifferentialSpec<P, Case, Outcome>,
  inputs: PortValues<P>,
  cases: readonly Case[],
  reuse: Reuse | undefined,
): (string | undefined)[] {
  if (!reuse || !spec.subjectKey) return new Array(cases.length).fill(undefined);
  const subject = inputs[spec.subject] as PortValue<P[keyof P]>;
  return cases.map((c, i) => {
    const sk = spec.subjectKey!(subject, c, inputs);
    return sk === undefined ? undefined : `${i}\u0000${sk}`;
  });
}

/**
 * Run a differential specification directly (tools, the workbench's Checks view) without a manager. IRWB-004: with
 * `baselineKey`, the cases and baseline outcomes are kept and reused; with `subjectKey` too, a case whose verdict is
 * known for its subject key is not evaluated again. The evidence is the same either way.
 */
export function runDifferential<P extends Ports, Case, Outcome>(
  spec: DifferentialSpec<P, Case, Outcome>,
  inputs: PortValues<P>,
  seed = spec.seed ?? 1,
  maxCounterexamples = spec.maxCounterexamples ?? 5,
): DifferentialEvidence {
  const { cases, reuse } = prepare(spec, inputs, seed);
  const keys = memoKeys(spec, inputs, cases, reuse);
  const verdicts: Decided[] = new Array(cases.length);
  for (let i = 0; i < cases.length; i++) {
    const known = keys[i] === undefined ? undefined : reuse!.verdicts.get(keys[i]!);
    if (known) {
      verdicts[i] = known;
      STATS.replayed++;
      continue;
    }
    verdicts[i] = decideCase(spec, inputs, cases[i], i, reuse?.outcomes);
    if (keys[i] !== undefined) reuse!.verdicts.set(keys[i]!, verdicts[i]);
  }
  return tally(spec, seed, cases, verdicts, maxCounterexamples);
}

/** `runDifferential`, with the cases nobody decided yet handed to `spec.decide` (IRWB-004 F4). */
export async function runDifferentialAsync<P extends Ports, Case, Outcome>(
  spec: DifferentialSpec<P, Case, Outcome>,
  inputs: PortValues<P>,
  seed = spec.seed ?? 1,
  maxCounterexamples = spec.maxCounterexamples ?? 5,
): Promise<DifferentialEvidence> {
  const { cases, reuse } = prepare(spec, inputs, seed);
  const keys = memoKeys(spec, inputs, cases, reuse);
  const verdicts: Decided[] = new Array(cases.length);
  const todo: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < cases.length; i++) {
    const known = keys[i] === undefined ? undefined : reuse!.verdicts.get(keys[i]!);
    if (known) {
      verdicts[i] = known;
      STATS.replayed++;
    } else todo.push(i);
  }
  const elsewhere = todo.length ? spec.decide?.(inputs, cases, todo) : undefined;
  if (elsewhere) {
    const decided = await elsewhere;
    if (decided.length !== todo.length) throw new Error(`${spec.id}: decide returned ${decided.length} of ${todo.length}`);
    todo.forEach((i, k) => (verdicts[i] = decided[k]));
    STATS.delegated += todo.length;
  } else for (const i of todo) verdicts[i] = decideCase(spec, inputs, cases[i], i, reuse?.outcomes);
  for (const i of todo) if (keys[i] !== undefined) reuse!.verdicts.set(keys[i]!, verdicts[i]);
  return tally(spec, seed, cases, verdicts, maxCounterexamples);
}

/** The assurance a check's evidence supports: sampled differential testing is weaker than an exact checker. */
export function assuranceOf(evidence: unknown): 'checked-sampled' | 'checked-exact' {
  return (evidence as { method?: string } | null)?.method === 'sampled' ? 'checked-sampled' : 'checked-exact';
}

